<h2 id="artificial-intelligence">Artificial intelligence</h2>
<p>Trying to keep pace with the changes in (AI) Artificial Intelligence is very difficult with the speed at which it is progressing. We already use AI in many facets of our lives to save time, innovate, and solve problems. The integration with AI and robotics, particularly drones will likely improve the safety and reliability of automated systems and streamline the processing of data to near real time. One of the biggest limitations of drones has been the ability to amass large amounts of data but a lack of resources to process and make meaning from that data. AI will likely decrease data processing time and allow automated systems to operate in ways we have yet to imagine. As with any new and emerging technology there will be failures and setbacks as systems are refined and problems are solved. This course will likely not touch on AI other than to look at new and emerging AI solutions as they relate to drones. Here are a few things to look for:</p>
<ul><li>
AI can be used for real-time image processing, which could allow drones to operate in areas without GPS such as indoor environments or areas where GPS has been jammed or denied in some way.
</li><li>
Swarm drones, such as those seen in drone light demonstrations. AI can help to coordinate and generate much more complex movements of large groups of drones.
</li><li>
Remote sensing platforms for wildfire detection, crop inspection, search and rescue and other applications could allow for rapid detection of desired targets (lost hikers, wildfire, crop damage) without the need for humans to manually examine images.
</li><li>
Drone warfare is already happening between Russia and Ukraine. As each side develops new and deadlier drones integrating AI, the dark side of AI development is becoming rapidly apparent.
</li></ul>
